{"id":"18bdcac2-07f2-42ae-8b1b-36b22c6db4d4","object":"chat.completion","created":1785022910,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate payload has required fields\\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing required fields: nodes and required_symbols')\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    if not nodes:\\n        raise ValueError('Empty node list')\\n    \\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {}  # id -> node dict\\n    children = {}   # id -> list of child ids\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node.get('id')\\n        if nid is None:\\n            raise ValueError('Node missing id')\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    # Populate children lists\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            if parent not in node_map:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Parent {parent} not found')\\n            children[parent].append(nid)\\n    \\n    # Check that all required symbols exist in some node\\n    available_symbols = {node['symbol'] for node in nodes}\\n    for sym in required:\\n        if sym not in available_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym} not present in any node')\\n    \\n    # Initialize retained set with all node ids\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    # Remove order list\\n    removed_order = []\\n    \\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then node id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n.get('depth', 0), n.get('id', '')))\\n    \\n    # Helper to get all descendant ids (including the node itself)\\n    def get_subtree_ids(nid):\\n        subtree = {nid}\\n        stack = list(children[nid])\\n        while stack:\\n            cid = stack.pop()\\n            if cid in subtree:\\n                continue\\n            subtree.add(cid)\\n            stack.extend(children[cid])\\n        return subtree\\n    \\n    # Process nodes\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue  # already removed\\n        # Check conditions for removal\\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        # Never remove root (parent is None and id usually 'root')\\n        if node.get('parent') is None:\\n            continue\\n        # Never remove a node whose symbol is required\\n        if node.get('symbol') in required:\\n            continue\\n        # Never remove a node with retained children\\n        has_retained_children = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_children:\\n            continue\\n        # Tentative removal: check that all required symbols remain\\n        # Symbols that would be lost: symbol of this node and its descendants\\n        subtree_ids = get_subtree_ids(nid)\\n        lost_symbols = set()\\n        for sid in subtree_ids:\\n            if sid in node_map:\\n                lost_symbols.add(node_map[sid]['symbol'])\\n        # Compute remaining symbols after removal\\n        remaining_symbols = set()\\n        for rid in retained:\\n            if rid not in subtree_ids:\\n                remaining_symbols.add(node_map[rid]['symbol'])\\n        # Check if all required remain\\n        if required.issubset(remaining_symbols):\\n            # Remove subtree\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n            retained.difference_update(subtree_ids)\\n    \\n    # Sorted retained node ids\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    # Removed order as list\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a solve(case) function that implements grammar_guided_node_reduction. The problem is about reducing a parse tree (nodes with parent references, symbols, removable flags, utility values, depth) while preserving required symbols. We need to decide which nodes to remove, retaining required nodes and ensuring all required symbols remain represented. The algorithm: deeper nodes before shallower, use node ID as tie breaker. Tentatively remove a node if it's removable, not required symbol, and all required symbols remain after removal. Also cannot remove a node with retained children? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That means we can only remove leaf nodes (no retained children). But we need to consider that if we remove a node, its children are also removed? In Perses-like reduction, removing a node removes its subtree. The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we consider removing a node (and its entire subtree) if after removal, the set of symbols present among retained nodes still includes all required symbols. Also must not remove root, required symbol nodes (those whose symbol is in required_symbols?), or nodes with retained children (i.e., nodes that have children that are not removed yet). So we process nodes in order of decreasing depth, and for each removable node that is not required symbol and is a leaf (or after its children are removed?), we check if removing it still leaves required symbols. If so, we remove it. The node IDs to remove are the IDs of the nodes we decide to remove. Retained nodes are all nodes not removed.\n\nWe need to return \"removed_node_ids\" in the order they were removed (presumably the order we process and decide to remove). \"retained_node_ids\" sorted (presumably alphabetical or whatever, but \"sorted retained IDs\" - output schema says array of strings, we need to sort them. The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted in some order (maybe lexicographic). We'll sort them ascending lexicographically as strings.\n\nWe need to build the tree from nodes list. Each node has id, parent (null for root), symbol, depth, removable, utility. We'll create a dict from id to node. Also map parent to children.\n\nWe need to identify required symbols list. Nodes can have same symbol; required_symbols is list of symbols that must appear at least once among retained nodes. We need to ensure that after removal, the set of symbols from all retained nodes covers required_symbols.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build adjacency (children) and map id->node.\n- Determine set of required_symbols.\n- Determine initial set of retained node ids (all nodes).\n- For each node in decreasing depth (and tie-break by node id ascending? The description: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort nodes by depth descending, then by node id ascending (as strings or as whatever). Because we want deeper nodes first, and if equal depth, use node id as tie breaker. Then for each node in that order:\n  - Check if node is removable (removable == True).\n  - Check if node id is root? root's parent is null and its symbol is \"root\"? But we can treat root as not removable anyway. Actually root's removable is false, so check passes.\n  - Check if node's symbol is in required_symbols? But wait: \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" - but a node with a symbol that is in required_symbols might be needed. However if there are multiple nodes with same symbol, it's okay to remove one as long as at least one remains. The instruction \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The phrase \"a required symbol\" could mean a node whose symbol is required, but if there are duplicates, removing one might be allowed if another remains. But the instruction says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So we must interpret that as: never remove a node if its symbol is in required_symbols? That would be too strict, because then we could never remove any node with a required symbol even if there are multiple copies. But the example: required_symbols: root, s1. root is not removable anyway. s1 appears in n1 and n4? n1 has symbol s1, n4 also has symbol s1. So we might need to remove n4? But n4 is removable. However if we consider s1 as a required symbol, then any node with symbol s1 cannot be removed. That would prevent removal of n4, even if n1 remains. That seems contradictory to typical Perses where you can remove a node if the required symbols still appear. The instruction \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" suggests that we check the entire set of retained symbols after removal. So it's okay to remove a node with a required symbol if another node with that symbol is retained. So the prohibition \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be ambiguous. But given the typical algorithm, we should interpret it as: never remove a node that is the only representative of a required symbol. But the algorithm using the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" handles that implicitly. However the instruction also says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" This is a separate list of never remove conditions. It might be that we must not remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols regardless of duplicates. Let's check the task name: grammar_guided_node_reduction. This is like Perses reduction. In Perses, you can remove a node if the resulting tree still covers all required tokens (or symbols). So it's not that you can't remove a node with a required symbol, but you can if there's another node with that symbol. However the instruction explicitly says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". Could be a mistake? But we must follow the hard contract. The phrase \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" might mean: do not remove a node that is the root, do not remove a node whose symbol is designated as required (i.e., required_symbols list), and do not remove a node that has children that are retained (i.e., not yet removed). That is a stricter condition. But then the condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" would be redundant if you never remove any required symbol node. However the condition could still apply: after removing nodes that are not required symbol nodes, you still need to ensure all required symbols remain represented (since required symbols might be carried by nodes that are not themselves required? Actually required symbols are symbols, not nodes. So if you never remove nodes with required symbols, then required symbols always remain. So the check is unnecessary. But the instruction lists both, so we should apply both: never remove a node if its symbol is in required_symbols (i.e., symbol of that node is in required_symbols list). Similarly, never remove a node that has children that are still retained. So we'll filter nodes accordingly: only consider nodes that are removable, not root, symbol not in required_symbols, and have no retained children (i.e., all its children are either already removed or non-existent). Since we process deeper nodes first, children will be considered before parents, so if a node has children that are still retained, we skip it. That's consistent.\n\nNow, after removal, we must update retained set. But we only need to track removal order and final retained set.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Build mapping from id to node object (dict).\n2. Build children mapping: for each node, id -> list of child ids (by parent field).\n3. Determine set of required_symbols.\n4. Determine all node ids.\n5. Maintain set of retained ids (all initially).\n6. Maintain list of removed ids in order.\n7. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by node id lexicographically ascending (as strings). Since node ids are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc., we can compare as strings.\n8. For each node in that order:\n   - If node.id not in retained: continue (already removed, maybe descendant? but we process removal order, so if we remove a node, its children are also removed? Actually when we remove a node, its entire subtree is removed. So if we remove a node, all its descendants become removed as well. But we process nodes individually. To handle that, after removing a node, we should also remove all its descendants from retained. But the algorithm may not need to individually process descendants because they will be encountered later but already removed. We can simply remove the node and its subtree from retained. Since we process in depth order, a parent will be processed after its children, so if a child was removed earlier, the parent's children set will be empty (or not retained) and we can consider removing parent. So we need to track retained set globally; when we remove a node, we remove it and all its descendants from retained. So we need a function to recursively remove subtree when a node is removed.\n   - Check conditions:\n     - Node is removable (removable field is True). (Root has removable false, so automatically passes?)\n     - Node's symbol is not in required_symbols.\n     - Node has no children that are retained (i.e., all children ids are not in retained set). Since we are processing deeper first, children should already be either removed or retained? If a child is removable and we decided to keep it (because we didn't remove it), then the parent has a retained child and cannot be removed. So we skip.\n   - If all conditions pass, then tentatively check if removing this node (and its subtree) would still leave all required symbols represented. That means after removal, the set of symbols of all retained nodes (excluding this node and its descendants) must include all required_symbols. We can compute current retained symbol set from retained ids (after removing previous nodes). Then compute symbols that would be lost: the node's symbol plus symbols of all its descendants (since they are removed with it). Check if each required symbol is still present after removing those symbols. If yes, then we remove the node: add its id to removed_node_ids, and remove the node and its entire subtree from retained set.\n9. After processing all nodes, the remaining retained set is the set of IDs that were never removed.\n10. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids (in order), \"retained_node_ids\": sorted list of retained ids (ascending lexicographically)}.\n\nEdge cases: root cannot be removed because removable false and symbol root is in required_symbols. required_symbols might contain symbols not present? Probably not. If a required symbol is not present in any node, then the problem is infeasible? The instructions say raise ValueError for infeasible. So we should check that initially, all required symbols are present in at least one node.\n\nWe also need to handle the fact that some nodes are not removable. They will never be considered.\n\nNow produce the implementation in Python.\n\nImportant: The function must be named solve and take case as argument. Must not mutate case. Must return a JSON-serializable object.\n\nWe'll write the code inside the JSON response under \"implementation\".\n\nMake sure to use only standard library.\n\nNow produce the final JSON response.\n\nNote: The output schema requires \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\" as arrays of strings. We'll ensure sorted for retained.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":3314,"total_tokens":5486,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2420},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}